            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Italian for to put in a plaster cast, then the Italian for to limp."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: slogarsi",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "slogarsi — \"to sprain, to dislocate\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Giocando a tennis mi sono slogato la caviglia. Playing tennis I sprained my ankle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Slogarsi la caviglia — to sprain one's ankle; in speech also prendere una storta."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "slogarsi is built from s- \"out of\" and luogo \"place\", inherited from Latin locus (Lewis & Short): a joint put out of its place."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
